  "text": "**Solution 2 is correct.** Its threshold is $q^* = \\frac{N+\\alpha+4}{N}$.\n\nThe key step is the scaling of the cross term $\\int (I_\\alpha * |u|^{\\bar p})\\,G(u)$, where $\\bar p = \\frac{N+\\alpha}{N}$ and $G(u) \\sim |u|^q$ near $0$. Take $u = t\\,w(x/L)$ with fixed mass $s = t^2L^N$. The gradient term scales as $sL^{-2}$, and the cross term scales as $s^{(\\bar p+q)/2}L^{-\\frac{N}{2}(q-\\bar p)}$. The threshold is where the two $L$-exponents match:\n\n$$2 = \\tfrac{N}{2}(q-\\bar p) \\implies q^* = \\bar p + \\tfrac{4}{N} = \\tfrac{N+\\alpha+4}{N}.$$\n\nThis also passes the admissibility check. The value lies in $\\left(\\frac{N+\\alpha}{N}, \\frac{N+\\alpha}{N-2}\\right)$ exactly when $\\alpha > N-4$, which is the hypothesis $\\alpha > \\max\\{0, N-4\\}$ in the problem. That match is strong evidence the threshold is right.\n\nThe other two fail:\n\n- **Solution 1** uses the pure Choquard mass-critical power $\\frac{N+\\alpha+2}{N}$. That ignores the mixed structure of $F$ and the cross term that drives the threshold.\n- **Solution 3** makes the same mass-critical choice. Its admissibility claim is also wrong: $\\frac{N+\\alpha+2}{N} < \\frac{N+\\alpha}{N-2}$ holds only when $\\alpha < 2$, not for all $\\alpha > -2$.\n\nCHOICE: 2",
